        {
          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01XAYT8Wdd3tQHX5UcKKtnUK",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022concepts/flag-debt.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Flag Debt\\ndescription: The accumulation of unreturned feature flags that silently multiply code paths and testing burden.\\ntimestamp: 2026-09-30T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Flag-Debt\\n  - concept/Testing\\n  - concept/Technical-Debt\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Single authoritative source describing the core problem and cleanup discipline.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\nFlag debt is the operational risk that emerges when feature flags are not removed after their rollout completes. Each unreturned flag represents a code path that must be tested, understood, and maintained indefinitely, even after the feature it guarded is long since stable in production.\\n\\n## The Problem\\n\\nWhen a feature flag remains in the codebase after the feature has been fully rolled out and is stable, it becomes **technical debt**. Unlike other forms of debt, flag debt is particularly insidious because:\\n\\n- **Silent multiplication of code paths**: The number of possible execution paths grows exponentially with each unreturned flag. Two flags create 4 paths; ten flags create 1024.\\n- **Testing burden**: Each new code change must now be tested against multiple flag combinations, dramatically increasing test matrix size and complexity.\\n- **Maintenance friction**: Developers must understand and reason about code that may never execute in production, introducing cognitive overhead.\\n- **Unclear intent**: Over time, it becomes unclear which flags are still needed, which have been forgotten, and which are candidates for cleanup.\\n\\n## Cleanup Discipline\\n\\nEffective flag management requires a **cleanup discipline**: a systematic practice of removing flags after their rollout completes. This includes:\\n\\n- **Explicit removal criteria**: Define when a flag is ready to be removed (e.g., \\u0022after 30 days in production at 100%\\u0022, \\u0022once metrics confirm stability\\u0022).\\n- **Ownership tracking**: Assign a team or owner to each flag, with accountability for its eventual removal.\\n- **Scheduled review**: Periodically audit flags in the codebase to identify candidates for cleanup.\\n- **Automated tooling**: Use flags-as-a-service platforms that surface stale flags and automate the removal process where possible.\\n\\nWithout discipline, flag debt accumulates silently and compounds \\u2014 each new feature adds more paths to the matrix without reducing existing ones.\\n\\n## Relationship to [[concepts/deploy-release-decoupling]]\\n\\nFeature flags enable the operational capability to decouple deployment from release, allowing code to ship dark and then be exposed gradually. That decoupling is valuable during rollout. However, the value of that decoupling is lost if the flag remains in the codebase indefinitely after rollout completes \\u2014 at that point, it becomes pure debt with no operational benefit.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: eval://steering-adoption/sample-09 \\u2014 Feature flag operational risk and flag debt cleanup discipline\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
      "assistant_text": "Perfect. No existing page covers feature flags or flag debt. The operator\u0027s task framing explicitly asks me to focus on **flag debt and its cleanup discipline**, and to skip the deploy/release decoupling part since it\u0027s covered elsewhere.\n\nNow I\u0027ll create a page on flag debt. Based on the source:\n- The core concept is **flag debt** \u2014 unreturned/unremoved feature flags that multiply code paths and testing burden\n- The cleanup discipline is the practice/discipline of removing flags after rollout completes\n- I should acknowledge the deploy/release decoupling capability but not duplicate that coverage\n\nLet me create the page:",
